<Project>

	<!--
		One version per package for the whole solution.

		Before this file the three lanes pinned three different Core versions (0.1.8, 0.1.9, 0.1.8),
		which is how the web lane's unrestorable references stayed invisible: nothing in the build
		compared them. Versions now live here and nowhere else, so a mismatch is impossible to write.

		CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled keeps a transitive dependency from quietly pulling in
		an older Core than the one declared here.

		The rule for this file is about the RELEASED showroom: when it is released, every version below
		has to be on nuget.org, because the showroom is what a stranger clones first and it must restore
		from the public feed alone. Getting that wrong is how the web lane once shipped references to a
		Core and a Config that existed only in a local feed.

		While the chain is being developed, the local feed (artifacts/nuget-local, wired up by the
		NuGet.config one directory above this repository) is what serves it, and pinning a version that
		exists only there is the normal way one package consumes another's work in progress. What must
		not happen is the reverse: publishing to nuget.org in order to make a pin here resolve.

		So this list is a statement of where the chain currently is, not a defect to go and fix:

		  Core            0.4.0 pinned, local feed  (0.3.0 is the newest released)
		  Config          0.3.0 pinned, local feed  (0.2.1 released)
		  Simple          0.3.0 pinned, local feed  (0.2.1 released)
		  Azure           0.4.0 pinned, local feed  (0.3.0 released)
		  LocalIO         0.4.0 pinned, local feed  (0.3.0 released)
		  Container       0.4.0 pinned, local feed  (0.3.0 released)
		  Container.Azure 0.4.0 pinned, local feed  (0.3.0 released)
		  Container.Web   0.4.0 pinned, local feed  (0.3.0 released)
		  UI.Browser      0.1.0 pinned, local feed  (nothing released yet)
		  Web             0.4.0 pinned, released    (the one already on the public feed)

		Two consequences worth knowing rather than discovering. A clone of this repository on its own
		does not restore while a pin is local-only - it needs the sibling repositories and the feed built
		from them. And the numbers here are the numbers that will be published, so when the chain is
		released this file needs no edit: nothing in it is a placeholder. Release order, when someone
		decides to release, is dependency order: Core, Simple, Config, LocalIO, Azure, Web, Container,
		Container.Azure, Container.Web, then UI.Browser.
	-->

	<PropertyGroup>
		<ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>
		<CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>true</CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<ItemGroup Label="TestFramework">
		<PackageVersion Include="TestFramework.Azure" Version="0.4.0" />
		<PackageVersion Include="TestFramework.Config" Version="0.3.0" />
		<PackageVersion Include="TestFramework.Container" Version="0.4.0" />
		<PackageVersion Include="TestFramework.Container.Azure" Version="0.4.0" />
		<PackageVersion Include="TestFramework.Container.Web" Version="0.4.0" />
		<PackageVersion Include="TestFramework.Core" Version="0.4.0" />
		<PackageVersion Include="TestFramework.LocalIO" Version="0.4.0" />
		<PackageVersion Include="TestFramework.Simple" Version="0.3.0" />
		<PackageVersion Include="TestFramework.UI.Browser" Version="0.1.0" />
		<PackageVersion Include="TestFramework.Web" Version="0.5.0" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup Label="Test host">
		<PackageVersion Include="coverlet.collector" Version="6.0.0" />
		<PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="17.8.0" />
		<PackageVersion Include="xunit" Version="2.5.3" />
		<PackageVersion Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="2.5.3" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup Label="Applications under test">
		<PackageVersion Include="Azure.Data.Tables" Version="12.9.1" />
		<PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.ApplicationInsights.WorkerService" Version="2.23.0" />
		<!-- 3.59.0, not the 3.57.1 the Function App used to pin: TestFramework.Azure 0.2.0 needs
		     at least 3.59.0, and one central version means the higher floor wins for both. -->
		<PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Azure.Cosmos" Version="3.59.0" />
		<PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Azure.Functions.Worker" Version="2.1.0" />
		<PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Azure.Functions.Worker.ApplicationInsights" Version="2.0.0" />
		<PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Azure.Functions.Worker.Extensions.Http.AspNetCore" Version="2.0.2" />
		<PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Azure.Functions.Worker.Extensions.ServiceBus" Version="5.24.0" />
		<PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Azure.Functions.Worker.Extensions.Timer" Version="4.3.1" />
		<PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Azure.Functions.Worker.Sdk" Version="2.0.5" />
		<PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Data.SqlClient" Version="6.1.2" />
		<PackageVersion Include="Newtonsoft.Json" Version="13.0.4" />
	</ItemGroup>

</Project>
